he wreckage as he could and  together that they did  on their way home from their successful  expedition jennings and vane encountered  a spanish ship they raided it too stole  the treasure aboard and went on their  way but selling their loot was  complicated the world had again turned  against the pirate  gone were the days of unrestrained  buccaneering against the spanish  this was peacetime and london saw to it  that its british colonies disavowed  jennings and the other pirates during  the war of spanish succession england  the dutch republic and portugal had  rallied against france over the rightful  heir to the childless spanish king  charles ii  bloodshed and conquest had returned to  the caribbean and the privateers roamed  free  but now the winds had turned yet again  and the debaucherous doors of port royal  were closed to stolen goods and maritime  outlaws  if men like henry jennings and charles  vane were going to become pirates they  needed friendly ports and a new base of  operations  only a few years removed from war the  bahamian island of new providence and  its city of nassau sat scarred and  nearly abandoned green and red-throated  parrots warbled above decaying forts  which hadn't flown british colours in  years  frothy waves lapped against white shores  and leaning palm trees nassau also  boasted a natural harbour that could  hold a hundred ships located near trade  routes and the wreckage of the treasure  fleet this was the scene the former  privateer benjamin hornigold stumbled  upon a new eden bereft of any semblance  of government  hornigold quickly assumed control and  gave the pirates exactly what they  needed jennings vane and thousands of  other disaffected sailors were welcomed  into the pirates paradise of nassau  the pirates clambered ashore with  pistols and swords and faces freckled  with scurvy spots here they spent their  ill-gotten wealth on rolls of dice  rounds of rum and seedy nights in  run-down brothels  chests of immaculate gold and silver  from the wreckage made their way into  town  in the throes of treasure fever the  pirates indulged in the lawler success  from the fortified pools of nassau  captains could launch attacks on passing  merchant vessels  in the commonwealth of pirates he who  goes to the greatest length of  wickedness is looked upon with a kind of  envy amongst them as a person of more  extraordinary gallantry and is thereby  entitled to be distinguished by some  post and if such a one has but courage  he must certainly be a great man  the pirates attacked merchant ships  hauling in tobacco rum cotton tar sugar  and slaves before returning to the  safety of nassau englishmen attacked  englishmen without regard for common  heritage bold brutality became a badge  of pride  but for all its anarchical trappings  nassau came to be governed in a sense by  two rival pirate factions  one under henry jennings and the other  under benjamin hornigold hornigold's  rising second in command edward teach or  blackbeard also held sway despite this  top-down tyranny had no place in nassau  in a period when monarchies were  widespread and common sailors languished  under draconian captains for poor wages  this so-called republic of pirates  embraced fraternity and  proto-egalitarianism in its undaunting  pursuit of wealth half a century before  the eloquent democratic treatises of  payne and jefferson the pirate ship  became a primordial site of defiance  toward absolutism and monarchy  indeed the pirates of this era made  decisions collectively voted out  incompetent and unfair captains and  shared their loot  many followed pirate codes that harshly  penalized disloyalty and the cheating of  one's own crew while many still reserved  harsh violence for their victims pirates  like blackbeard spurned violence deemed  unnecessary cultivating instead an aura  of fear to force victims into  surrendering without resistance  the pirate flag or jolly roger was  intended for just this purpose the red  or black flag often flew emblazoned with  icons of death skulls bones and  hourglasses  pirates used flags strategically tailing  target vessels and hoisting the feared  emblem of piracy when close enough to  make good on their implicit threat to  unleash carnage  [Music]  but by 1726 king george the first had  had enough from the opulence of his  palace he made a dramatic move to  annihilate the pirate threat  and conquer nassau  his plan was to fracture whatever unity  these pitiful enemies of the state  professed his majesty had issued a royal  pardon in this carrot and stick approach  pirates who surrendered to the proper  authorities in the coming year would be  pardoned and free to carry on with their  lives but those who rejected this  proposal would in turn receive a price  on their head and a royal promise that  they would be hunted down arrested and  killed  when news from new york of the impending  pardon reached the shores of nassau  hundreds of pirates rejoiced henry  jennings and benjamin hornigold and many  other former privateers had never  intended to be reviled pirates prophet  had possessed them but now they saw a  fortuitous escape to the world of  respectable commerce amidst their  euphoria the pro-pardoned pirates  restored the union jack atop the fort of  nassau  but there was a schism  hundreds of men refused to return to the  mundane poverty of civility those who  embraced the life of a ship-borne outlaw  and the bloody quest for fortune and  leisure these pirates including edward  england christopher billy onehand  condont and calico jack rackham now  found a leader in the most die-hard  anti-pardoned pirate of them all  charles  vane vane rallied his supporters and  staged an armed takeover of the fort he  tore down the union jack and raised high  the grinning face of death the black  flag for the time being nasser would  remain the unapologetic property of  pirates a brazen island of liberty chaos  and wealth  in their defiance of